--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Earlier sessions of the same course
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Days 4 and 5 of this work reuse material from the earlier course sessions.

  The exercises themselves are the course organisers' material, written in R
  against lidR and terra:

    Lecture2_Exercise_SurfaceModels.Rmd    ground, DTM, CHM, surface models
    Session3_Exercise_Segmentation.Rmd     tree detection and crown segmentation

  They are not distributed here and belong to the organisers at SLU.

  pcf (point_cloud_forestry) is the present author's own Python mirror of that
  R pipeline, written for those sessions and reused here rather than rewritten.
  It lives in a separate repository and is an optional dependency. On the ALS
  crown step it outperforms the implementation in this repository and is what
  the pipeline now uses.

  The algorithms reached through it belong to their authors, not to pcf:

    lidR                     Roussel J.-R., Auty D. et al. (2020)
                             Remote Sensing of Environment 251:112061
                             doi:10.1016/j.rse.2020.112061
    Dalponte 2016 crowns     Dalponte M. & Coomes D.A. (2016)
                             Methods in Ecology and Evolution 7:1236-1245
                             doi:10.1111/2041-210X.12575
    Silva 2016 crowns        Silva C.A. et al. (2016)
                             Canadian Journal of Remote Sensing 42:554-573
                             doi:10.1080/07038992.2016.1196582

  Two further methods are implemented directly in this repository from their
  published descriptions:

    Kozak variable-exponent taper   Kozak A. (2004)
                                    Forestry Chronicle 80(4):507-515
    Log back-transform correction   Baskerville G.L. (1972)
                                    Canadian Journal of Forest Research 2(1):49-53

